WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 24: The Leadville Ice Palace of 1896: The Crystal Fortress of the Rockies

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.560
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:13.680
[Rebecca] Perched two miles above sea level in the rugged Colorado Rockies, Leadville was once the undisputed silver capital of the world.

4
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:24.880
[Rebecca] But in 1893, the repeal of the Sherman Silver Purchase Act collapsed silver prices, shuttering dozens of mines and plunging the booming mountain community into desperate poverty.

5
00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:31.200
[Rebecca] Facing starvation and mass unemployment, the eccentric citizens of Leadville refused to surrender.

6
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:38.880
[Rebecca] In the freezing winter of 1895, they rallied together to construct the most audacious architectural Wonder the Frontier had ever seen.

7
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:44.400
[Rebecca] A colossal medieval castle built entirely out of 5,000 tons of solid ice.

8
00:00:44.400 --> 00:01:00.560
[Rebecca] Spanning five acres and featuring electric lights frozen inside translucent blue walls, a grand ballroom, and curling rinks, the Leadville Ice Palace was a dazzling triumph of frontier optimism until an unpredictable warm wind changed everything.

9
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.280
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

10
00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.360
[George] But the Gilded Age came crashing down in the summer of 1893.

11
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:45.280
[George] A catastrophic national financial panic swept the United States, prompting Congress and President Grover Cleveland to repeal the Sherman Silver Purchase Act.

12
00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:53.520
[George] Overnight, the federal government ceased its mandatory purchases of silver, and the price of silver plummeted by more than 50%.

13
00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.400
[George] For Leadville, the effect was apocalyptic.

14
00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.800
[George] Within months, 30 major mines shut down their steam pumps and locked their headframes.

15
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:16.840
[George] Over 4,000 miners and smelter workers lost their jobs, commercial banks failed, grocery stores boarded up their windows, and bitter sub-zero mountain winds whistled through empty streets as destitute families faced freezing starvation.

16
00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:23.720
[George] The Colorado Mountain boomton seemed destined to become the largest and most tragic ghost town in the American West.

17
00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:33.000
[George] In the autumn of 1895, as a second brutal winter of depression loomed over the Rockies, a charismatic local mining investor named Tingley S.

18
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.920
[George] Wood proposed an outlandish scheme to save the city from extinction.

19
00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.280
[George] Wood had traveled to Montreal, Canada, and St.

20
00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:47.400
[George] Paul, Minnesota, where winter carnivals had successfully used temporary ice architecture to attract tourists.

21
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:57.480
[George] Wood asked a bold question: why shouldn't Leadville capitalize on its most abundant, inescapable natural resource, its ferocious, sub-zero winter cold.

22
00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:04.440
[George] At 10,000 feet elevation, Leadville experienced freezing temperatures that lasted from October through May.

23
00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:20.920
[George] Wood proposed constructing a colossal, permanent winter palace constructed entirely out of solid blocks of natural lake ice, which would host an international winter carnival, bringing wealthy tourists and passenger trains from across the nation to flood the local economy with cash.

24
00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:24.600
[George] The town embraced the eccentric vision with religious fervor.

25
00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:36.920
[George] Local merchants, mine owners, and even out-of-work miners donated their last savings, raising nearly $40,000 in stock subscriptions to form the Leadville Crystal Carnival Association.

26
00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.560
[George] Prominent Denver architect Charles E.

27
00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:47.440
[George] Joy was hired to design a magnificent Norman-style medieval castle that would surpass anything ever built in Europe or Canada.

28
00:03:47.760 --> 00:04:02.720
[George] Joy spent weeks sketching soaring Romanesque arches, medieval battlements, and octagonal towers, calculating the structural compressive strength of mountain lake ice to ensure the massive frozen walls would not buckle under their own crushing weight.

29
00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:08.080
[George] Construction began in November 1895 under punishing sub-zero conditions.

30
00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:14.480
[George] Nearly 300 unemployed miners and carpenters were hired to cut and haul the frozen building materials.

31
00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:27.040
[George] Over 5,000 tons of pristine, crystal-clear blue ice were harvested from nearby mountain lakes and reservoirs, cut into massive blocks measuring 20 inches thick and weighing 200 pounds each.

32
00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:32.000
[George] Another 2,000 tons of crystal ice were shipped by rail from Palmer Lake.

33
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.560
[George] The scale of the construction was staggering.

34
00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:43.280
[George] The ice palace covered a colossal five acres of land on Capitol Hill, measuring 400 feet long by 325 feet wide.

35
00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:55.760
[George] Its centerpiece was a massive Norman keep flanked by two towering octagonal battlements rising 90 feet into the Alpine sky, complete with crenellated turrets and foe cannons carved from solid ice.

36
00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:05.200
[George] To construct the towering walls, carpenters built an interior framework of heavy timber beams, which were then encased on both sides with ice blocks.

37
00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:16.640
[George] Between the blocks, workers sprayed water from fire hoses in the freezing night air, fusing the seams into a seamless, impenetrable wall of solid ice eight feet thick at the base.

38
00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:31.560
[George] Electrician crews laid thousands of feet of insulated wiring, freezing hundreds of incandescent light bulbs directly inside the translucent ice blocks to illuminate the palace in glowing shades of crimson, emerald green, and sapphire blue.

39
00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:36.200
[George] The freezing temperatures posed unprecedented challenges for the electricians.

40
00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:47.000
[George] Wire insulation cracked in the subzero wind, and glass bulbs had to be carefully tempered in cold water before being sealed into the slush seems to prevent sudden thermal shattering.

41
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:57.000
[George] On New Year's Day, 1896, the Leadville Ice Palace threw open its frozen portals to the world, and it was nothing short of an alpine fairy tale.

42
00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:12.040
[George] Inside the freezing citadel lay a breathtaking indoor wonderland, a grand ballroom boasting a polished wooden dance floor warmed by charcoal brasiers, where a full brass orchestra played waltzes for dancing couples wrapped in heavy beaver fur coats.

43
00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:22.680
[George] Adjacent to the ballroom was a massive indoor skating rink spanning 15,000 square feet, surrounded by grandstands and illuminated by crystal ice chandeliers.

44
00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:29.960
[George] In the exhibition halls, local merchants created whimsical displays encased permanently within blocks of solid ice.

45
00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:39.560
[George] Frozen mountain trout swimming in ice blocks, taxidermy bighorn sheep, sides of beef, and even bottles of champagne frozen in crystal columns.

46
00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:52.760
[George] Outside, visitors cheered at curling matches, watched dog sled races, and plunged down a 300-foot wooden toboggan slide that sent thrill seekers hurtling across the frozen valley at 50 miles an hour.

47
00:06:52.760 --> 00:07:08.040
[George] Special tourist trains operated by the Denver and Rio Grande Railroad brought tens of thousands of visitors up the steep mountain passes, and for a few glorious weeks, Leadville was once again the vibrant, laughing cultural capital of the American West.

48
00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:23.360
[George] Over 5,000 visitors packed into the crystal Halls on opening night alone, marveling as electric lights embedded within the ice walls glowed like frozen sapphires and rubies against the subzero Colorado sky, while brass bands played until dawn.

49
00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:26.880
[George] Yet the magnificent triumph was tragically short-lived.

50
00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:33.440
[George] In mid-February 1896, nature played a cruel, unprecedented joke on the cloud city.

51
00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:40.080
[George] An anomalous warm weather pattern, a powerful, balmy Chinook wind, descended over the Rocky Mountains.

52
00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:47.120
[George] For two weeks, unseasonable spring temperatures soared into the sixties under a relentless, blinding mountain sun.

53
00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:49.680
[George] The crystal fortress began to weep.

54
00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:53.360
[George] Massive ninety-foot towers began to lean precariously.

55
00:07:53.360 --> 00:08:03.840
[George] Decorative battlements softened into rounded blobs of slush, and water poured from the ceilings in continuous torrents, turning the polished ballroom floor into a ruined swamp.

56
00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:14.240
[George] Engineers scrambled to cover the southern facade with heavy canvas tarpaulins and blankets to deflect the sun, but the structural integrity of the timber framing was compromised.

57
00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:25.920
[George] Fearing that the massive 90-foot towers would collapse and crush visitors, carnival directors made the agonizing decision to close the palace doors on March 28, months ahead of schedule.

58
00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:36.000
[George] When the carnival closed its books, the association had suffered a heavy financial loss, leaving investors with empty pockets and debts that would take years to settle.

59
00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:44.560
[George] Throughout the spring and summer of 1896, the skeletal remains of the ice palace slowly dissolved into the mud of Capitol Hill.

60
00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:55.920
[George] The massive wooden timbers were salvaged and sold to shore up underground mine tunnels, while the remaining ice finally melted away in the July sun, leaving behind an empty hillside.

61
00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:05.400
[George] The town never attempted to build another ice palace, and within months, a bitter, violent miners' strike erupted that further tore the community apart.

62
00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:13.800
[George] Yet, more than a century later, the Leadville Ice Palace remains one of the most beloved and celebrated legends in Colorado history.

63
00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:29.240
[George] It was a shining monument born not from wealth, but from desperation, a testament to the unquenchable frontier spirit of a community that, when faced with total ruin, chose to gather its frozen tears and build a palace of dreams two miles in the sky.

64
00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:37.960
[George] The heartbreak of the melting ice palace in March 1896 was merely the prelude to an even darker storm that enveloped the Cloud City.

65
00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:49.080
[George] Just weeks after the Crystal Fortress dissolved into slush on Capitol Hill, the long simmering economic misery of Leadville's working class erupted into full-scale industrial warfare.

66
00:09:49.080 --> 00:10:03.960
[George] In June 1896, 1,200 members of the Western Federation of Miners declared a general strike across the Lake County Mining District, demanding a standard wage of $3 a day to offset the devastating silver price crash.

67
00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:16.520
[George] Mine owners retaliated by forming a ruthless employer's syndicate, importing armed strikebreakers from Missouri, and fortifying their mine headframes with rifle parapets and high pressure hot water hoses.

68
00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:28.840
[George] On September 21, striking miners armed with Winchester rifles assaulted the Coronado and Emmett mines in a ferocious midnight firefight that claimed five lives and set the surface buildings blazing.

69
00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:38.680
[George] Colorado Governor Albert McIntyre declared martial law, deploying the Colorado National Guard equipped with gatling guns to occupy Leadville for six months.

70
00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:53.280
[George] Although the strike was eventually crushed, the resilience forged during the construction of the Ice Palace and the Labor War cemented Leadville's reputation as the most unbreakable, fiercely independent mining community in Rocky Mountain history.

71
00:10:53.920 --> 00:11:02.880
[Rebecca] The Leadville Ice Palace of 1896 reminds us that human creativity and resilience shine brightest when the odds are stacked impossibly high.

72
00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:07.760
[Rebecca] Even when palaces melt and dreams dissolve, the courage to build them endures.

73
00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:10.720
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

74
00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:12.000
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

75
00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:16.400
[Rebecca] Join us next Tuesday as we continue our journey across the American story.

76
00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:18.960
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

